---
import { getEntry } from "astro:content";
import ProjectLayout from "@/layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
import ProjectMedia from "@/components/ui/ProjectMedia.astro";
import TindaTrackEngineering from "@/components/sections/TindaTrackEngineering.astro";
const project = (await getEntry("projects", "tindatrack"))!;
const chapters = [
  {
    id: "sell",
    title: "Record a sale.",
    description:
      "The interface collects products, quantities, and a payment method. The server validates the order and returns the recorded transaction.",
    roles: ["sell", "receipt"],
  },
  {
    id: "track",
    title: "Follow the inventory.",
    description:
      "Signed movements connect stock changes to the operation that caused them. Audit records add the actor, time, and affected entity.",
    roles: ["track", "audit"],
  },
  {
    id: "review",
    title: "Review the day.",
    description:
      "Daily reporting brings completed transactions and their line items into one view. These screenshots contain demonstration data.",
    roles: ["review"],
  },
];
---

<ProjectLayout project={project}>
  <div class="container-main">
    {chapters.map((chapter, index) => (
      <section id={chapter.id} class="case-chapter">
        <div class="case-chapter-header" data-reveal>
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">
              0{index + 1} / {chapter.id}
            </p>
            <h2>{chapter.title}</h2>
          </div>
          <p>{chapter.description}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="case-gallery">
          {project.data.gallery
            .filter((media) => chapter.roles.includes(media.role))
            .map((media) => (
              <ProjectMedia
                src={media.src}
                alt={media.alt}
                caption={media.caption}
                sizes="(min-width: 1280px) 1100px, 90vw"
              />
            ))}
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>
  <div id="engineering">
    <TindaTrackEngineering />
  </div>
  <section class="container-main section prose">
    <h2>Decisions and tradeoffs</h2>
    <p>
      A modular monolith keeps business rules in the service layer without the
      deployment complexity of distributed services. A movement ledger preserves
      traceability, at the cost of deriving inventory from history rather than
      reading a freely editable stock field.
    </p>
    <p>
      Serializable transactions protect the sale boundary under contention.
      Retry logic handles retryable conflicts, while insufficient stock remains
      a validation failure. Historical sale prices are stored on line items so
      later price changes do not rewrite receipts.
    </p>
    <h2>Validation and limitations</h2>
    <p>
      The repository contains frontend workflow tests and backend integration
      tests, including competing sales for the last unit. Source and test code
      were reviewed during planning; the TindaTrack test suite was not executed
      for this portfolio. The example above shows the test’s expected invariant,
      not a new benchmark result.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a single-store MVP and demonstration deployment. It does not claim
      real customers, business impact, accounting completeness, or
      production-scale load testing. Accounting, payroll, e-commerce, and
      multi-tenant SaaS are outside its scope.
    </p>
    <h2>What the project demonstrates</h2>
    <p>
      Interface simplicity depends on clear system boundaries. Server-owned
      prices, atomic sales, and traceable stock changes let the interface stay
      focused on the user's task while preserving evidence behind each
      operation.
    </p>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
<style>
  .case-gallery {
    display: grid;
    gap: 32px;
  }
  .case-gallery :global(img) {
    max-height: 740px;
    width: 100%;
    object-fit: contain;
  }
</style>
